### What is the Triplets with Smaller Sum problem?

Given an array of integers and a target value, count how many triplets (i, j, k) with i < j < k have a sum strictly less than the target. The brute-force cubic enumeration is too slow for large arrays.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class TripletsWithSmallerSum {
    public static int countTriplets(int[] arr, int target) {
        Arrays.sort(arr);
        int count = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length - 2; i++) {
            int left = i + 1, right = arr.length - 1;
            while (left < right) {
                int sum = arr[i] + arr[left] + arr[right];
                if (sum < target) {
                    count += right - left; // batch count
                    left++;
                } else {
                    right--;
                }
            }
        }
        return count;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] fees = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
        int cap = 10;
        System.out.println("Triplets under cap: " + countTriplets(fees, cap));
    }
}
```

```text
Triplets under cap: 4
```

### Why does the batch counting trick work?

The array is sorted. When arr[i] + arr[left] + arr[right] is less than the target, then arr[i] + arr[left] paired with any element from arr[left+1] through arr[right] also gives a sum less than the target, because those elements are smaller than or equal to arr[right]. The number of such valid pairs is right - left.

